# What may enter a build context, and the shape is deny-first on purpose.
#
# The build context is the repository root, because the control plane imports
# `../../../src/token.js` and friends by literal relative path and the layout
# cannot be flattened. So the context is a directory that also holds, on the
# machine this was written on:
#
#   * `.env` at mode 0600 — the daemon's REEMOAT_TOKEN,
#   * `packages/control-plane/.env`,
#   * `*.db` files, if REEMOAT_DB was ever pointed here,
#   * 439 MB of node_modules whose pnpm bin shims have /Users/rends/... baked
#     into an exported NODE_PATH, so they are wrong in an image anyway.
#
# All of those are gitignored, so none is in a fresh clone and every one is on
# the machine most likely to run the first build. Deny-first rather than a list
# of exclusions, because a missed exclusion puts a live credential in a layer
# somebody can `docker save`, while a missed inclusion is a build error you see
# immediately.
#
# The `!src/*.ts` lines are named individually rather than `!src/`, and that is
# the coupling made visible: those files are the whole of what the control plane
# reaches for out of the root, and their transitive closure is `node:crypto` plus
# `hono`. `!src/` would drag the daemon's ACP surface in behind them. An import
# not named here fails the build, which is the right failure.
#
# ⚠ **This list and `deploy/docker/Dockerfile`'s COPY lines must agree**, and the
# failure when they do not is worth stating because it does not name this file:
# an entry missing *here* fails at COPY with `"/src/<name>": not found`, because
# the build context never carried it, while an entry missing only from the
# Dockerfile fails later at `Cannot find module`. Two lists, one fact — adding an
# import means editing both.

*

!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!tsconfig.json

!src/token.ts
!src/auth.ts
!src/cors.ts
!src/http.ts
!src/relay/protocol.ts

!packages/control-plane/package.json
!packages/control-plane/src
!packages/control-plane/scripts

!deploy/docker/prune-store.mjs

# Served by `GET /install.sh`, with this instance's own address substituted into
# it — so a self-hosted control plane hands out an installer that points at
# itself. Its twin is the COPY line in deploy/docker/Dockerfile's runtime stage;
# missing here the build fails at COPY with "not found", missing there the route
# answers 404 and nothing else notices, which is why `imagecheck` asks for it
# from a real container.
!deploy/bootstrap.sh

!packages/web/package.json
!packages/web/tsconfig.json
!packages/web/vite.config.ts
!packages/web/index.html
!packages/web/src
# The favicon and the touch icon. Vite copies this directory to the root of
# `dist` verbatim, and `index.html` names both files — so without this the page
# ships with two `<link>`s pointing at 404s, which is what happened the first
# time: the build succeeded, `dist` was correct on the build host, and the
# container served JSON errors for `/favicon.svg`.
!packages/web/public

# Re-deny what a directory allow just let back in. A `!packages/web/src` above
# would otherwise readmit a stray `.env` or a `dist` somebody built in there.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.log
**/.DS_Store
